(d) a foreign credit rating agency incorporated in a Financial Action Task Force (FATF) member jurisdiction and recognised under their law, having a minimum of five years' experience in rating securities;  
(e) any company or body corporate having continuous net worth of minimum rupees one hundred crores as per audited annual accounts for the previous five years.

Under Regulation 14 of SEBI (CRA) Regulations, 1999, a written agreement between a credit rating agency and its client must contain:
(a) the rights and liabilities of each party;
(b) the fee to be charged;
(c) the client shall co-operate for periodic review;
(d) the client shall provide true, adequate and timely information;
(e) the CRA shall disclose the rating assigned to the client's securities irrespective of whether the rating is accepted by the client;
(f) the client shall disclose all ratings assigned in the last three years and any unaccepted rating.

A credit rating agency must maintain the following records for a minimum period of five years:
(a) copy of its balance sheet;
(b) a copy of its profit and loss account;
(c) a copy of the auditor's report;
(d) a copy of the agreement entered into with each client;
(e) information supplied by each client;
(f) correspondence with each client;
(g) ratings assigned, including upgradation and downgradation;
(h) rating notes considered by the rating committee;
(i) record of decisions of the rating committee;
(j) letter assigning rating;
(k) particulars of fees charged.

